# Review contents survive the detector comparison

Document ID: `reiyah.perception-admission.checkpoint-note`

Version: `0.1.0`

Lifecycle status: `exploratory`

Can reviewed evidence constrain a detector comparison without silently losing a disputed
proposal or a joint interpretation? The new admission path checks the selected review contents,
accounts for every proposal in every supplied world, and generates the conditional reference
input. Constructed tests preserve opposite conclusions caused by a disputed base-neighbor
object. A separate two-window example retains its shared choices and yields the exact mean
loss-difference interval [0,0]. Making joint coverage unknown restores [-1,1]. Omitting a
proposal produces an error and no admission report.

These are synthetic computations, not new detector-performance measurements. The two real
development windows remain unresolved at [-8,8] under unit penalties, with zero human judgments.
No cohort or seed was selected, no real assisted review was performed and Gate A is unaccepted.

## What changed and why

The existing point-reference compiler accepts explicitly assumed worlds whose evidence digests
are opaque identifiers. A valid digest shape does not show what a source says. The
[new module](../tools/perception_admission.py) adds a contents-checked entry point: it freshly
checks [comparison/observation binding](PERCEPTION_BINDING_CHECKPOINT_2026-09-10.md), verifies two
selected sealed discovery records, validates separately identified assisted proposals and reads
a bounded adjudication ledger. The [interface](../research/perception-admission/0.1.0/README.md)
states the complete source, chronology, accounting and trust boundaries.

The conventional solution is an explicit relational join and per-world disposition table.
It needs no new optimizer, perception model or majority-vote algorithm. Each proposal is
qualified by its canonical record digest and local ID. Every world accounts for every proposal
in its own window, including unresolved or explicitly excluded entries. Object coordinates,
class, time, members and rationale contribute to the derived evidence identities. Complete
original records and disagreements remain available in the private report, including objects
that never become graph nodes because they are unresolved or outside the target/range.

The tool cannot establish that the author supplied every physically plausible interpretation.
Finite world coverage and exclusion of unlisted objects remain separate explicit assumptions.
Incomplete inspection and unresolved dispositions open the affected window; unknown discovery
blinding or missing chronology opens the joint model. Distinct handles and consistent reported
times never establish human identity, independence or actual sealing before exposure.

The existing lower-level reference interface remains available for explicitly assumed models;
it does not thereby acquire reviewed-source admission. Existing matching, additive loss,
detector normalization, physical-study rules and old evidence bytes are unchanged.

## Verification and retained corrections

The final repository suite passes 283 tests, including 24 new admission tests. The 93 measurement
tests pass against unchanged measurement code. These are 376 engineering checks, not independent
scientific review. The [machine checkpoint](../research/perception-admission/0.1.0/checkpoint.json)
binds code, tests, source closure, captured receipts and the synthetic demonstration.

The tests attack fabricated basis fields, altered coordinates with stale expected identities,
omitted adverse worlds, missing/foreign/repeated proposals, reused record IDs, unassigned drafts,
wrong package and predecessor identities, forged summaries, partial inspection, unresolved class
or time, missing coverage, resource exhaustion, source changes during compilation and output reuse.
They compare tiny matching cases with a separately structured enumeration of partial injections.
Certificate checking runs with the producer's matcher disabled in the admission tests.

Internal review found a chronology gap after the first passing test run. When assisted completion
was unrecorded, an adjudication dated before the discovery seals was admitted as an open model.
It did not produce a false finite conclusion, but contradicted the invalid-versus-unresolved
contract. A failing regression was captured before correcting the consumer to compare every
known predecessor order, even when the intermediate time is absent. Review also removed repeated
serialization of complete proposal evidence from per-world object hashing; fixed-size source
digests now retain the same transitive binding.

The first private demonstration command failed before executing the example because its import
path did not include the isolated export. Its script and failed capture remain retained. The
corrected launcher ran the full constructed chain: two byte-identical admission reports,
separately verified finite/open packets, and a retained omitted-proposal rejection. The complete
small demonstration took 0.75 seconds wall time with 54,968,320 bytes maximum resident set size
on the recorded local runtime. This is one resource observation, not a performance benchmark.

## Limits and next useful check

Admission verifies selected bytes and formal source relationships. It does not authenticate a
coherently fabricated review history, prove an adjudicator's physical interpretation, validate
nominal sensor calibration, or establish statistical confidence coverage. Original submitted
file digests inside sealed discovery records remain producer assertions unless those original
bytes are supplied. Shared trusted components are named in the interface and report.

The next value check is an observed review workflow with a perception-validation lead and a
competent conventional analyst receiving the same staged evidence and mathematical tools.
Record effort, corrections, disagreement and the decision each can support. Prepare a bounded
rehearsal on already exposed development evidence before any prospective cohort selection;
do not manufacture reviewers or use synthetic records as physical-study evidence. The proposed
60-scene study retains its independent-review, adjudication and method-freeze prerequisites.

This checkpoint establishes an inspectable path from declared review contents to a checked
conditional comparison. Physical validity, a changed integration decision and repeated product
value remain open obligations.
